// Compiled by bin/build-help.php from help/*.md (the add-on's topics only). Do not edit.
export default {"format":"cadcraft-help","version":1,"app":"modelcraft","addon":"twin","name":"Digital twin","sections":{"start":"Getting started","tasks":"Walkthroughs","switching":"Coming from other software","reference":"Reference","trouble":"Troubleshooting and limits"},"topics":[{"slug":"twin-start","scope":"addon:twin","title":"Digital twin: getting started","summary":"Turn a Material flow layout into a working plant you can watch, test and compare.","section":"start","order":10,"keywords":["digital twin","emulation","simulation","virtual commissioning","plc","conveyor","throughput","factory"],"headings":[{"id":"what-it-is","text":"What it is","level":2},{"id":"first-five-minutes","text":"First five minutes","level":2},{"id":"what-each-part-does","text":"What each part does","level":2},{"id":"not-a-safety-validation","text":"Not a safety validation","level":2}],"html":"\u003Ch2 id=\"what-it-is\"\u003EWhat it is\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EDigital twin\u003C/span\u003E is an add-on for ModelCraft that sits beside \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EMaterial flow\u003C/span\u003E. It reads the same layout. Nothing has to be remodelled.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"first-five-minutes\"\u003EFirst five minutes\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EOpen \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESettings\u003C/span\u003E, then \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EAdd-ons\u003C/span\u003E, and switch on \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EDigital twin\u003C/span\u003E and \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EMaterial flow\u003C/span\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EIn the \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EDigital twin\u003C/span\u003E panel, choose a cell under \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EStart from a cell\u003C/span\u003E and press \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EUse this cell\u003C/span\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EUnder \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESee it run\u003C/span\u003E, press \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ERun\u003C/span\u003E, then \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EPlay\u003C/span\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EChoose what each station is: a machine, a robot, a robot with a pallet, or a person at a bench.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"what-each-part-does\"\u003EWhat each part does\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESee it run\u003C/span\u003E draws the equipment, plays the run, and gets it out as video, for the path tracer, or as animated USD.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ETest the controls\u003C/span\u003E runs your PLC program on the model with faults and repeatable tests.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EWill the load ride?\u003C/span\u003E rides a load along a line with physics and checks each belt against the formulas.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ECompare layouts\u003C/span\u003E runs versions of the layout side by side and ranks what is limiting the line.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EBill of materials\u003C/span\u003E lists quantities from the layout.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"not-a-safety-validation\"\u003ENot a safety validation\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EAn emergency stop here is a test input. No result here proves a safety function.\u003C/p\u003E","text":"What it is Digital twin is an add-on for ModelCraft that sits beside Material flow. It reads the same layout. Nothing has to be remodelled. First five minutes Open Settings, then Add-ons, and switch on Digital twin and Material flow. In the Digital twin panel, choose a cell under Start from a cell and press Use this cell. Under See it run, press Run, then Play. Choose what each station is: a machine, a robot, a robot with a pallet, or a person at a bench. What each part does See it run draws the equipment, plays the run, and gets it out as video, for the path tracer, or as animated USD. Test the controls runs your PLC program on the model with faults and repeatable tests. Will the load ride? rides a load along a line with physics and checks each belt against the formulas. Compare layouts runs versions of the layout side by side and ranks what is limiting the line. Bill of materials lists quantities from the layout. Not a safety validation An emergency stop here is a test input. No result here proves a safety function."},{"slug":"twin-controls","scope":"addon:twin","title":"Digital twin: test the controls","summary":"Run a PLC program on the model with no hardware, force signals, inject faults and run repeatable tests.","section":"tasks","order":20,"keywords":["plc","soft plc","structured text","plcopen","iec 61131-3","force","fault","test","commissioning","modbus","emergency stop"],"headings":[{"id":"give-the-program-something-to-drive","text":"Give the program something to drive","level":2},{"id":"the-program","text":"The program","level":2},{"id":"run-it","text":"Run it","level":2},{"id":"tests","text":"Tests","level":2},{"id":"a-real-plc","text":"A real PLC","level":2}],"html":"\u003Ch2 id=\"give-the-program-something-to-drive\"\u003EGive the program something to drive\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ESet a stop gate&#039;s mode to \u003Ccode\u003Eplc\u003C/code\u003E in \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EMaterial flow\u003C/span\u003E. A gate in that mode stays shut until the program opens it. Photo-eyes become inputs.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"the-program\"\u003EThe program\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EStarter program\u003C/span\u003E writes the declarations for every signal and a first rule for every gate. The address of each signal is in the \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESignals\u003C/span\u003E list. \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EOpen a program\u003C/span\u003E takes a \u003Ccode\u003E.st\u003C/code\u003E file or a PLCopen XML file exported from your controller tool. Keep the addresses from the list, as signals connect by address.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"run-it\"\u003ERun it\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EStart\u003C/span\u003E runs the program in step with the model. Use \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EForce\u003C/span\u003E on a signal to hold it on or off. Use \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EFaults\u003C/span\u003E to stop a belt or a station, stick a gate or a photo-eye, or press the emergency stop, then \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EClear\u003C/span\u003E it. The status line says when the line has stalled.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"tests\"\u003ETests\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ERun the tests\u003C/span\u003E runs the tests the layout gets by itself: ten minutes without a stall, a belt stopping and restarting, a station going down and coming back, the emergency stop closing every PLC gate, and a stuck photo-eye. It runs in simulated time. The same program and seed repeat to the byte, so a failure can be run again and shown to someone. \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESave the report\u003C/span\u003E saves the result as CSV.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"a-real-plc\"\u003EA real PLC\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EMaterial flow\u003C/span\u003E lists Modbus TCP addresses, and CadCraft Studio serves them while the model runs live. Other PLC protocols are not built yet.\u003C/p\u003E","text":"Give the program something to drive Set a stop gate's mode to plc in Material flow. A gate in that mode stays shut until the program opens it. Photo-eyes become inputs. The program Starter program writes the declarations for every signal and a first rule for every gate. The address of each signal is in the Signals list. Open a program takes a .st file or a PLCopen XML file exported from your controller tool. Keep the addresses from the list, as signals connect by address. Run it Start runs the program in step with the model. Use Force on a signal to hold it on or off. Use Faults to stop a belt or a station, stick a gate or a photo-eye, or press the emergency stop, then Clear it. The status line says when the line has stalled. Tests Run the tests runs the tests the layout gets by itself: ten minutes without a stall, a belt stopping and restarting, a station going down and coming back, the emergency stop closing every PLC gate, and a stuck photo-eye. It runs in simulated time. The same program and seed repeat to the byte, so a failure can be run again and shown to someone. Save the report saves the result as CSV. A real PLC Material flow lists Modbus TCP addresses, and CadCraft Studio serves them while the model runs live. Other PLC protocols are not built yet."},{"slug":"twin-output","scope":"addon:twin","title":"Digital twin: pictures, video and other programs","summary":"Three ways to get a run out, from a quick video to a path-traced clip or animated USD.","section":"tasks","order":30,"keywords":["video","render","path tracer","usd","unreal","blender","animation","proposal"],"headings":[{"id":"three-levels-of-picture","text":"Three levels of picture","level":2},{"id":"what-this-does-not-do","text":"What this does not do","level":2}],"html":"\u003Ch2 id=\"three-levels-of-picture\"\u003EThree levels of picture\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EThe live view.\u003C/strong\u003E \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EDraw as equipment\u003C/span\u003E shows frames, rollers, legs, machines, robots and cartons, and \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESave a video of this view\u003C/span\u003E records it. Fast, shaded drawing. The shapes are generic, for the picture.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EThe path tracer.\u003C/strong\u003E \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EPut the run in the model\u003C/span\u003E adds the equipment as real geometry and the loads on the animation timeline. Open \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ERender\u003C/span\u003E for path-traced stills and video. This is real quality, but not real time. Robot arms do not move in this version of the clip.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EAnother program.\u003C/strong\u003E \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESave animated USD\u003C/span\u003E writes a \u003Ccode\u003E.usda\u003C/code\u003E file with the equipment, materials and every load&#039;s movement. It is checked against OpenUSD. Programs that read USD can open it, including Unreal&#039;s USD Stage and Blender, and any render engine you already own can take over from there. We have not yet opened it in Unreal or Blender ourselves.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EStart at\u003C/span\u003E and \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESeconds\u003C/span\u003E set which stretch of the run goes out. Up to 150 loads go in the model or the USD file; the message says if any were left out.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"what-this-does-not-do\"\u003EWhat this does not do\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EReal-time rendering at the quality of a game engine is not available in the app. For that, use the USD file in your own copy of a game engine.\u003C/p\u003E","text":"Three levels of picture The live view. Draw as equipment shows frames, rollers, legs, machines, robots and cartons, and Save a video of this view records it. Fast, shaded drawing. The shapes are generic, for the picture. The path tracer. Put the run in the model adds the equipment as real geometry and the loads on the animation timeline. Open Render for path-traced stills and video. This is real quality, but not real time. Robot arms do not move in this version of the clip. Another program. Save animated USD writes a .usda file with the equipment, materials and every load's movement. It is checked against OpenUSD. Programs that read USD can open it, including Unreal's USD Stage and Blender, and any render engine you already own can take over from there. We have not yet opened it in Unreal or Blender ourselves. Start at and Seconds set which stretch of the run goes out. Up to 150 loads go in the model or the USD file; the message says if any were left out. What this does not do Real-time rendering at the quality of a game engine is not available in the app. For that, use the USD file in your own copy of a game engine."},{"slug":"twin-physics","scope":"addon:twin","title":"Digital twin: will the load ride, compare layouts","summary":"Physics on a load along a line, formulas per belt, and layouts compared with repeat runs.","section":"tasks","order":40,"keywords":["physics","friction","tip","slip","incline","curve","throughput","bottleneck","what-if","confidence interval","bill of materials"],"headings":[{"id":"will-the-load-ride","text":"Will the load ride?","level":2},{"id":"compare-layouts","text":"Compare layouts","level":2},{"id":"bill-of-materials","text":"Bill of materials","level":2}],"html":"\u003Ch2 id=\"will-the-load-ride\"\u003EWill the load ride?\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EEnter the load&#039;s size, mass and friction on the belt, then press \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ERide it\u003C/span\u003E. One to eight loads ride the line from a source with rigid-body physics, following the first output of each divert. It says whether they ride the line, tip, fall off, stop moving, or slip back. The table checks every belt with the formulas: slip on a slope at tan θ = μ, tip at tan θ = L/H, and slide on a curve at v²/r = μg.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EBoxes only. The rest of the plant stays on rules, as physics over a whole shift would take too long.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"compare-layouts\"\u003ECompare layouts\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EWrite one version a line, as \u003Ccode\u003Ename: item.setting=value\u003C/code\u003E, for example \u003Ccode\u003EFaster: station-1.servers=2, conveyor-1.speed=0.8\u003C/code\u003E. Item names are those in \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EMaterial flow\u003C/span\u003E. \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ECompare\u003C/span\u003E runs each version on the same random numbers. A difference counts only when its 95 % interval leaves out zero; otherwise it says \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ENo clear difference\u003C/span\u003E. More runs narrow the interval.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EMost likely limiting\u003C/span\u003E ranks stations by busy share minus time blocked by what follows. It is a place to look first, not a proof. \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EAdd as a version\u003C/span\u003E adds the obvious change for you to compare.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"bill-of-materials\"\u003EBill of materials\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EQuantities from the layout. Prices and suppliers are left for you to fill in.\u003C/p\u003E","text":"Will the load ride? Enter the load's size, mass and friction on the belt, then press Ride it. One to eight loads ride the line from a source with rigid-body physics, following the first output of each divert. It says whether they ride the line, tip, fall off, stop moving, or slip back. The table checks every belt with the formulas: slip on a slope at tan θ = μ, tip at tan θ = L/H, and slide on a curve at v²/r = μg. Boxes only. The rest of the plant stays on rules, as physics over a whole shift would take too long. Compare layouts Write one version a line, as name: item.setting=value, for example Faster: station-1.servers=2, conveyor-1.speed=0.8. Item names are those in Material flow. Compare runs each version on the same random numbers. A difference counts only when its 95 % interval leaves out zero; otherwise it says No clear difference. More runs narrow the interval. Most likely limiting ranks stations by busy share minus time blocked by what follows. It is a place to look first, not a proof. Add as a version adds the obvious change for you to compare. Bill of materials Quantities from the layout. Prices and suppliers are left for you to fill in."},{"slug":"twin-measured","scope":"addon:twin","title":"Digital twin: compare with a measured line","summary":"Put a file of measured part times beside the model, with the difference in units and as a percentage.","section":"tasks","order":50,"keywords":["measured","real line","validation","throughput","queue","ride time","csv","difference","percentage"],"headings":[{"id":"the-file","text":"The file","level":2},{"id":"what-is-compared","text":"What is compared","level":2},{"id":"what-this-does-and-does-not-show","text":"What this does and does not show","level":2}],"html":"\u003Cp\u003EUnder \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ECompare with a measured line\u003C/span\u003E press \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EOpen a measured file\u003C/span\u003E. The model is run on the same arrivals as the real line and both are read in exactly the same way, so each number can be set beside its twin.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"the-file\"\u003EThe file\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EA CSV with one row for every time a part passed a named point.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"ht-wrap\"\u003E\u003Ctable class=\"ht\"\u003E\u003Cthead\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EColumn\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EMeaning\u003C/th\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/thead\u003E\u003Ctbody\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003Epart, id or part_id\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EWhich part. The same text means the same part.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003Epoint, station or location\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EThe point it passed: the id of an item in the Material flow layout (the moment it entered that item), or the id followed by .out for the moment it left.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003Etime_s, time_min, time_h, time or clock\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EWhen. A column named just time uses the unit chosen above the button. clock is a time of day (h:mm:ss) counted from the first row.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/tbody\u003E\u003C/table\u003E\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EA part may pass a point once. If a row cannot be read, nothing is loaded and the panel shows the first problem with its line number; no row is skipped.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"what-is-compared\"\u003EWhat is compared\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EChoose a \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EFrom point\u003C/span\u003E and a \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ETo point\u003C/span\u003E. Time 0 is the first moment any part is seen at the from point. The window ends at the last part seen at the to point; anything the model does after that is left out of both. \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ELeave out the first (s)\u003C/span\u003E drops a warm-up from both lines.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EThroughput\u003C/strong\u003E: parts an hour passing the to point inside the window, and every point&#039;s own count.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EParts inside the stretch\u003C/strong\u003E: how many have passed the from point and not yet the to point, averaged over the window, and the most at once.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003ERide time\u003C/strong\u003E: the time from the from point to the to point of the parts that made it: mean, median, 95th percentile and spread.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EEach row shows the measured value, the model&#039;s, the model minus the measured value in the same unit, and that difference as a percentage of the measured value. \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESave the comparison\u003C/span\u003E writes the table as CSV.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EWhen the from point is a source, the model is given the measured arrival times (the orders mode of Material flow), so the difference shows what the model does with the arrivals the line really had. If it is not a source, the model runs on its own random arrivals and the panel says so.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"what-this-does-and-does-not-show\"\u003EWhat this does and does not show\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EIt reports how this model differs from this one measured stretch. It does not adjust the model, and it does not show the model is right for other days, other mixes or other points. How well the model matches a real line has to be judged on real data from that line; the checks run so far used a made-up file with known changes, which test the arithmetic and nothing more. This is not a safety validation.\u003C/p\u003E","text":"Under Compare with a measured line press Open a measured file. The model is run on the same arrivals as the real line and both are read in exactly the same way, so each number can be set beside its twin. The file A CSV with one row for every time a part passed a named point. Column Meaning part, id or part_id Which part. The same text means the same part. point, station or location The point it passed: the id of an item in the Material flow layout (the moment it entered that item), or the id followed by .out for the moment it left. time_s, time_min, time_h, time or clock When. A column named just time uses the unit chosen above the button. clock is a time of day (h:mm:ss) counted from the first row. A part may pass a point once. If a row cannot be read, nothing is loaded and the panel shows the first problem with its line number; no row is skipped. What is compared Choose a From point and a To point. Time 0 is the first moment any part is seen at the from point. The window ends at the last part seen at the to point; anything the model does after that is left out of both. Leave out the first (s) drops a warm-up from both lines. Throughput: parts an hour passing the to point inside the window, and every point's own count. Parts inside the stretch: how many have passed the from point and not yet the to point, averaged over the window, and the most at once. Ride time: the time from the from point to the to point of the parts that made it: mean, median, 95th percentile and spread. Each row shows the measured value, the model's, the model minus the measured value in the same unit, and that difference as a percentage of the measured value. Save the comparison writes the table as CSV. When the from point is a source, the model is given the measured arrival times (the orders mode of Material flow), so the difference shows what the model does with the arrivals the line really had. If it is not a source, the model runs on its own random arrivals and the panel says so. What this does and does not show It reports how this model differs from this one measured stretch. It does not adjust the model, and it does not show the model is right for other days, other mixes or other points. How well the model matches a real line has to be judged on real data from that line; the checks run so far used a made-up file with known changes, which test the arithmetic and nothing more. This is not a safety validation."},{"slug":"twin-behaviours","scope":"addon:twin","title":"Digital twin: give a part from a STEP file a job","summary":"Make a face of an imported part a conveyor surface, a joint or a sensor. The part is not changed; the job is saved with the file.","section":"tasks","order":60,"keywords":["step","imported cad","conveyor surface","joint","sensor","behaviour","face","axis","limit","photo-eye"],"headings":[{"id":"add-a-job","text":"Add a job","level":2},{"id":"test-a-job","text":"Test a job","level":2},{"id":"when-the-part-changes","text":"When the part changes","level":2},{"id":"what-this-does-not-do","text":"What this does not do","level":2}],"html":"\u003Cp\u003EAn imported part is just shape. Under \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EBehaviours on imported parts\u003C/span\u003E you tell the twin what a face of it does. The part is not edited. The job is saved in the model file and the run reads it.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"add-a-job\"\u003EAdd a job\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EImport a STEP file into ModelCraft.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EChoose the \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EJob\u003C/span\u003E: \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EConveyor surface\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EJoint\u003C/span\u003E or \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESensor\u003C/span\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EPress \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EPick a face\u003C/span\u003E and click a face of the part in the view. Esc stops picking.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EFill in the few numbers that job needs and press \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EAdd this job\u003C/span\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"ht-wrap\"\u003E\u003Ctable class=\"ht\"\u003E\u003Cthead\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EJob\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EThe face\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EYou set\u003C/th\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/thead\u003E\u003Ctbody\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EConveyor surface\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EA flat face that points up\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EWhich way it moves loads (along or across its long side, either way) and the speed in metres a second\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EJoint\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EA flat face (its normal is the axis) or a round face (its axis)\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ETurning or sliding, the lowest and highest value, where it starts, the rate. Degrees for turning, metres for sliding\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ESensor\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EA flat face\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EHow far it looks out, in metres. The beam leaves the face along its normal\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/tbody\u003E\u003C/table\u003E\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EA conveyor surface is treated as the smallest rectangle that holds the face, along the direction you chose. A face that points down is refused, and so is one tilted across the direction (the direction has to run along the slope). The message says which.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"test-a-job\"\u003ETest a job\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ETest\u003C/span\u003E runs it with the load size and friction from \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EWill the load ride?\u003C/span\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003EA conveyor surface: a load starts at rest at the near end and is timed to the far end, beside the time the friction formula gives. A load already at belt speed is timed too.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EA joint: moved to its highest value at its rate; it stops at its limit and says so.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EA sensor: a load is put on the first conveyor surface and the beam reports when it breaks and clears.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe solver carries a load about 3 % faster than the belt, so times can come out a little short of the formula.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"when-the-part-changes\"\u003EWhen the part changes\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EIf you import an edited version of the part, press \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ECheck against the part again\u003C/span\u003E. A job finds its face again when the same plane or axis is in the same place. It says whether the face was found as it was, found under a new number, or found with a different size. If no face fits, or two fit equally well, the job stays in the file, does nothing, and says why. Pick the face again to repair it. Nothing is guessed.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"what-this-does-not-do\"\u003EWhat this does not do\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EJoints are tested on their own; they do not yet move the part in the view or carry other parts. Jobs are not yet read by the Material flow run: they run in the physics check on the part itself. Round faces that were cut into uneven pieces on import read as plain &quot;other&quot; faces and cannot be a joint axis. This is not a safety validation.\u003C/p\u003E","text":"An imported part is just shape. Under Behaviours on imported parts you tell the twin what a face of it does. The part is not edited. The job is saved in the model file and the run reads it. Add a job Import a STEP file into ModelCraft. Choose the Job: Conveyor surface, Joint or Sensor. Press Pick a face and click a face of the part in the view. Esc stops picking. Fill in the few numbers that job needs and press Add this job. Job The face You set Conveyor surface A flat face that points up Which way it moves loads (along or across its long side, either way) and the speed in metres a second Joint A flat face (its normal is the axis) or a round face (its axis) Turning or sliding, the lowest and highest value, where it starts, the rate. Degrees for turning, metres for sliding Sensor A flat face How far it looks out, in metres. The beam leaves the face along its normal A conveyor surface is treated as the smallest rectangle that holds the face, along the direction you chose. A face that points down is refused, and so is one tilted across the direction (the direction has to run along the slope). The message says which. Test a job Test runs it with the load size and friction from Will the load ride?. A conveyor surface: a load starts at rest at the near end and is timed to the far end, beside the time the friction formula gives. A load already at belt speed is timed too. A joint: moved to its highest value at its rate; it stops at its limit and says so. A sensor: a load is put on the first conveyor surface and the beam reports when it breaks and clears. The solver carries a load about 3 % faster than the belt, so times can come out a little short of the formula. When the part changes If you import an edited version of the part, press Check against the part again. A job finds its face again when the same plane or axis is in the same place. It says whether the face was found as it was, found under a new number, or found with a different size. If no face fits, or two fit equally well, the job stays in the file, does nothing, and says why. Pick the face again to repair it. Nothing is guessed. What this does not do Joints are tested on their own; they do not yet move the part in the view or carry other parts. Jobs are not yet read by the Material flow run: they run in the physics check on the part itself. Round faces that were cut into uneven pieces on import read as plain \"other\" faces and cannot be a joint axis. This is not a safety validation."}]};
